  "query_preview": "..\nToday and Tonight: It will be a transition period. A ridge will\nbe nudging into the region behind the exiting Low. It will \npromote a dry ,warming trend from west to east through the day. \nNortheast WA and North Idaho Panhandle will have lingering \nshower activity through the mid morning potentially bringing \nanother 0.1-0.2 of precip. Any shower activity will diminish by \nthe early afternoon. Highs will start a upward trend from \nMonday. Highs for the day will be in the 70s. Overnight lows \nwill be in the 40s to low 50s. \n\nWednesday and Thursday: Winds will be main concern Wednesday \nand Thursday. For Wednesday, South to North winds will bring \nbreezy conditions across the Waterville Plateau and Okanogan \nValley. Sustained winds will be in the teens with gusts up to 25\nmph. Thursday will begin the breakdown of the stable pattern as\na trough begins to press into the region from BC/Alberta. It \nwill bring a tightening pressure gradient and stronger winds. \nFor Thursday, westerly winds spread across the Basin with \nsustained in the teens with gusts up to 30 mph. Locally higher \nwinds are possible over the Waterville Plateau with gusts up to \n40 mph. Blowing dust could be a concern in the area. Other \nimpacts include choppy waters and fire concerns that missed on \nlast weekends rain. These will be the warmest days of the week. \nHighs will climb back into the upper 70s and low 80s. Overnight \nlows will be in the upper 40s and 50s on Wednesday night. \nThursday night will be cooler in the 40s. Sheltered areas in the\nnorthern valleys could dip into the upper 30s.\n\nFriday and Saturday: A Low will push through the region as the \ntrough continues to deepen over the Pacific Northwest. A weak \nwave moves across Thursday night into Friday, then the Low digs \ndown the coast Friday night into early Saturday before swinging \ninland Saturday afternoon into Sunday. A few showers are \npossible around the mountains with the first wave, but better \nchances expand out Friday night into Saturday, before starting \nto retreat to the mountains and wane Sunday into Monday. Some \nmoderate rains are possible around the mountain zones Saturday, \nwith lighter amounts elsewhere. Embedded t-storms are possible \nand may produce locally higher amounts. Saturday is also \nforecast to be another breezy day. Temperatures cool to the \nupper 60s and 70s Friday, mostly 60s and localized low 70s \nSaturday, before moderating into Sunday and Monday. Lows will \ncontinue to be in the 40s. Sheltered areas in the northern \nvalleys could dip into the upper 30s. /JDC",
